// Copyright 2026 Particular LLC. MOHIO(TM) is a trademark of Particular LLC.
// Password login -- hash a password, check a login attempt against the stored
// hash, and grant a role on success. This is the real, already-built way to
// log a user in: no mioauth needed, these three primitives are it.
// Run it:  mio run cookbook/password-login.mho   (uses SQLite in memory)
//
// The password below is hardcoded on purpose, for a reproducible demo -- mio
// check correctly flags it. In a real app the value being hashed and the
// value being checked both come from request input, never a literal.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TWO SECURITY PROPERTIES BELOW, AND BOTH ARE LOAD-BEARING. Please do not
// "simplify" either one away when you copy this: each was a real, measured
// leak in an earlier version of this very recipe.
//
// 1. ONE ANSWER FOR BOTH FAILURES. A wrong password and an email nobody has
//    registered give the IDENTICAL message. The earlier version said "no
//    account with that email" for one and "wrong password" for the other,
//    which lets anyone check whether an address is registered here simply by
//    trying it. That turns a login form into a membership list, and for some
//    sites the membership list is the sensitive part.
//
// 2. THE SAME WORK EITHER WAY. When no account is found, the check below
//    still verifies the attempt against a throwaway hash. That looks
//    pointless and is not: verifying a password is deliberately slow, so a
//    version that skips it when the account is missing answers a stranger
//    far faster than it answers a member. Measured on the earlier version of
//    this recipe, the miss came back 182 times faster, which is just as
//    readable an answer as the message was.
//
// Together they mean every failed login costs the same and says the same.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

connect db as sqlite from env.DATABASE_URL

hash "correct-horse-battery-staple" as stored_hash using bcrypt

save to db.members
    email "alice@example.com"
    hashed_password stored_hash
    role "member"
save: done

// The decoy exists only to be verified against when no account is found, so a
// miss does the same slow work a hit does. Nothing ever logs in with it.
hash "this-value-is-never-anyone-password" as decoy_hash using bcrypt

retrieve.one member from db.members
    match email to "alice@example.com"
retrieve.one: done

// No account with that email finds NOTHING, and finding nothing is a normal empty
// result, not an error. So the test is `is empty`. `on.failure` means the database
// itself went wrong, and putting the not-found message there leaves it never printed
// while the code below reads a member who was never found.
check member
    when member is empty
        // Verified against the decoy purely so this path costs what the other
        // path costs. The answer is the same either way.
        check "correct-horse-battery-staple" against decoy_hash
            on.success
                show "invalid email or password"
            on.failure
                show "invalid email or password"
    otherwise
        check "correct-horse-battery-staple" against member.hashed_password
            on.success
                grant role member.role
                show "login ok, role granted: {{ member.role }}"
            on.failure
                show "invalid email or password"
check: done
